00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the pandemic changed office work  more employees are working from home  than ever before  but as the world begins to recover from  covid19  should things go back to how they were  do office workers belong in the office  and where are they most productive  [Music]  now callum and and and fingers can i  just ask you how your working lives have  changed in the last 18 months and  if you think ahead sort of covered  restrictions aside what does your ideal  new work model sort of looks like i  guess like everyone really i've moved  pretty much to a full time working from  home um i'm in some i'm in san francisco  at the moment uh which is where i i live  at the moment so in terms of what i  would ideally like it's pretty much this  i suppose the only difference would be  it would be better to have more  opportunities for kind of  the sort of back and forth of ideas that  uh was more common in the days of um  100 office going and for jira what's it  been like um for you i mean if i'm not  mistaken you changed jobs in the middle  of all of this yes i actually  interviewed for this job remotely and  i'm based in the london office and i  think  a lot's been said about the difficulty  of hiring and onboarding during  lockdowns but i think i've been  pleasantly surprised by the way that  it's worked out really well you know if  you think about your first day or your  first week in a new job you're nervous  you're in a brand new building with a  brand new commute and you know you're  just trying to remember everyone's names  i think for me uh being able to do that  from a familiar setting is actually i  think taken some of that pressure off i  wondered if you could just talk us  through  how  employees are feeling and how employers  are feeling i think this has been a huge  challenge for employers and they're  definitely mismatches and expectations  you know what employers want doesn't  always align with what employees expect  and if you look at apple you know i  think that's probably been the most high  profile case  senior managers want staff to return to  the office at least three days a week  but you're seeing that employees are  really pushing back and i believe uh  there have been two petitions that have  been signed by over a thousand people  a handful of people have actually  resigned over this at apple and uh you  know some employees are speaking out  publicly and i think that's that's  really striking because to have apple  employees speaking out so publicly  against their management is almost  unprecedented for a company that's been  so fiercely private it's now becoming a  cultural norm that uh if you are working  in a what you know knowledge intensive  work white collar work basically that  you will be allowed to  spend at least some of the week at home  and a company that is saying we want  everyone in the office five days a week  we'll quite quickly run up against a  problem where  good employees say i don't want to work  here anymore today most people envision  some form of hybrid working and gartner  predicts that globally half of all  knowledge workers will be remote at  least one day a week by the end of this  year and you know of course there will  be differences in preference parents and  carers for example are more likely to  want to work from home a number of days  a week  you've got disabled workers who reported  finding remote working more accessible  what i found most striking is how  race can impact that preference and this  is still an under researched area but  there was one report by slack which  showed that just three percent of  knowledge workers black knowledge  workers in america  wanted to return to the office full time  now that's compared to 21 of their white  counterparts a lot of workers are  talking about you know  not just of good work life balance but  also actually a really productive time  whereas quite a few employers  particularly those who are calling for  people to come back a lot seem to think  that actually you do need to physically  be somewhere to be productive the best  mix is not everyone in the office all  the time and it's also not everyone at  home all the time it's sort of a bit of  both um and that is because quite simply  you can have a more efficient division  of labor of your working week if you do  it that way so say for instance if i'm  working from home two days a week  on those two days i can do the tasks  that really require me to be like by  myself concentrating not interrupted and  and so on so i can do those on monday  and friday and on tuesday wednesday and  thursday i can do the kind of tasks that  um that require collaboration with  others should it just be left to workers  to figure out  how much they individually want to come  in or is it actually from an employer's  point of view more helpful to say  everyone should come in on a tuesday and  a thursday or everyone in this team  should come in on tuesday and thursday  and beyond that you know see for  yourself teams choosing which days they  want to come in uh is actually quite  helpful and i think it's something that  solves the issue of hybrid meetings you  know which could be a really big  challenge uh already we're seeing that  there's a lack of consensus around which  meetings people should hold in person  and which ones uh people should keep  virtual i mean something i'm hearing  more about over here is is the idea of  mandating days when you're not allowed  to come into the office  uh so you actually are you you must work  from home on tuesday for instance and  the reason the reason for that is  because um  it's sort of to try and overcome the  biases in sort of uh promotion and and  and that kind of thing that you might  get otherwise the original study on this  of which was done by on the chinese call  center workers showed that even if  people were equally productive  merely going into the office was enough  to get yourself a promotion basically  which is which is bad really  i think the challenge now for companies  is to try to find a middle ground that  works for all of their employees but  more crucially one that works for the  companies themselves  hi this was part of a digital event  produced exclusively for subscribers to  the economist if you'd like to watch the  full discussion of this or of one of our  other events then click the link you'll  just have to register if you haven't  already or you can become an economist  subscriber to enjoy access to all of our  journalism thanks for watching

